<h2>The gap in Section 19</h2>
<p>The Contempt of Courts Act, 1971 provides a statutory appeal under Section 19 only against an order or decision of a High Court "in the exercise of its jurisdiction to punish for contempt". The Supreme Court has consistently read that narrowly: until punishment is imposed, there is nothing to appeal under the Act. The practical consequence is familiar to every litigator who has defended a compliance affidavit — a contempt court can reject the affidavit, summon senior officers in person, and functionally re-adjudicate the original dispute, all through orders that the statute treats as unappealable. The only recourse has been a special leave petition under Article 136, an expensive and discretionary remedy.</p>

<h2>How the case arose</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Writ order</strong> — on 17 March 2026, the Allahabad High Court quashed a 2020 office memorandum and directed that the respondents\' candidature for the post of Assistant Prosecution Officer be considered.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Compliance decision</strong> — the competent authority considered and rejected the candidature on 2 September 2026, and filed a compliance affidavit in the contempt proceeding the respondents had instituted.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Contempt-stage order</strong> — the Single Judge rejected the compliance affidavit, directed personal appearance of the officer and indicated that charges might be framed if the writ order was not complied with.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Article 136</strong> — the officer, conceding that no special appeal lay under the High Court Rules, challenged the order directly before the Supreme Court.</div>
</div>

<h2>Civil contempt is not criminal jurisdiction</h2>
<p>The obstacle to an intra-court appeal was the exclusion, common to Letters Patent provisions and High Court rules, of orders passed in the exercise of "criminal jurisdiction". Contempt proceedings have often been loosely assimilated to criminal proceedings because of their quasi-penal character. The Bench declined to carry that assimilation over to civil contempt: proceedings under Section 12 for wilful disobedience of a judicial order are not resolved in criminal jurisdiction, and interim orders made before any punishment are not automatically barred from intra-court appeal on that ground. Decisions suggesting the contrary, the Court observed, may no longer be good law — hence the reference of <em>Ram Kishan Fauji v. State of Haryana</em> (2017) to a larger Bench.</p>

<h2>The four appealable situations</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Contempt court\'s action</th><th>Why an appeal may lie</th></tr>
<tr><td>Directions issued to persons who were not parties to the original order</td><td>The court creates new obligations rather than enforcing existing ones</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rejection of a compliance report coupled with a direction to comply afresh</td><td>The court substitutes its own view of the merits for the original adjudication</td></tr>
<tr><td>Orders travelling beyond the four corners of the order allegedly disobeyed</td><td>Contempt jurisdiction is corrective, not expansive</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decisions on disputed questions of rights between the parties</td><td>Such questions belong to substantive proceedings, not contempt</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The common thread is that the contempt court, in each situation, exercises a jurisdiction qualitatively different from testing wilful disobedience. Where the Letters Patent or the High Court\'s rules permit an intra-court appeal from orders of a Single Judge, these orders can be carried to a Division Bench without waiting for a punishment order.</p>

<h2>What practitioners should take from it</h2>
<p>For officers and parties facing contempt proceedings after genuine compliance, the judgment restores a remedy inside the High Court itself. Before rushing to the Supreme Court against an onerous contempt-stage direction, counsel should now examine the Letters Patent or the special-appeal rule of the High Court concerned and ask whether the impugned order falls within one of the four categories. In the case itself, the Supreme Court did not touch the merits: it granted the appellant a fortnight to file a special appeal, stayed the contempt proceeding for two months, and left all contentions open — a disposition that itself illustrates where the Court thinks such disputes belong.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The judgment is reportable, 2026 INSC 1128. The reference to a larger Bench on the correctness of Ram Kishan Fauji remains pending, so the position may be further clarified; orders of other High Courts will turn on the language of their own Letters Patent and rules.</p>
</div>
